\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. \emph  {Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the 403{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot  \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$. \emph  {Lower:} the same boundary in the units the search works in, where it is not flat, running 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz to 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation (0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). TRAPPIST--1 b{} is the one case the grid does not contain, missing by 0.01{} per cent.}}{10}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{6}{10}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. \emph {Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$. \emph {Lower:} the same boundary in the units the search works in, where it is not flat, running \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz to \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: Earth's rotation (\AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (\AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). \AccelWorstName {} is the one case the grid does not contain, missing by \AccelShortPct {} per cent}{figure.6}{}}
