\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Every stage-1 event and its fate: the 13{} windows that pass the detection threshold and the spatial screen, carried through the five stages of \S  \ref  {sec:statistic}. \emph  {screen}: the star outranks all 512{} controls, true of all by construction. \emph  {localised}: the visibility fit places the emission at the stellar position (\S  \ref  {sec:vistest}); 6{} do. \emph  {attributed}: the crossing lies within $\pm 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of a catalogued transition in the stellar frame with independent supporting evidence; 9{} do. \emph  {recurs}: the feature reappears at the same tuning in another block \emph  {and} satisfies there the same two conditions that define a stage-1 event, reaching the trigger and outranking that block's own controls; 7{} do and 1{} has no repeat coverage. The one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris window that fails is the Band\nobreakspace  {}6 tuning in which the resolved CO ring fills the control annulus and outshines the star, which is the behaviour extended emission produces and not a failure of the recurrence itself. 0{} events pass every stage. $^{a}$\nobreakspace  {}one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris block has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves and cannot be tested this way.}}{15}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:stages}{{6}{15}{Every stage-1 event and its fate: the \NStageTableRows {} windows that pass the detection threshold and the spatial screen, carried through the five stages of \S \ref {sec:statistic}. \emph {screen}: the star outranks all \NCtrl {} controls, true of all by construction. \emph {localised}: the visibility fit places the emission at the stellar position (\S \ref {sec:vistest}); \NStageLocalised {} do. \emph {attributed}: the crossing lies within $\pm \MaskVWidth $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of a catalogued transition in the stellar frame with independent supporting evidence; \NStageAttributed {} do. \emph {recurs}: the feature reappears at the same tuning in another block \emph {and} satisfies there the same two conditions that define a stage-1 event, reaching the trigger and outranking that block's own controls; \NStageRecurs {} do and \NStageNoRepeat {} has no repeat coverage. The one $\beta $~Pictoris window that fails is the Band~6 tuning in which the resolved CO ring fills the control annulus and outshines the star, which is the behaviour extended emission produces and not a failure of the recurrence itself. \NStageSurvivors {} events pass every stage. $^{a}$~one $\beta $~Pictoris block has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves and cannot be tested this way}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The 4{} stage-1 flagged windows with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics; $|\dot  \nu |_{\rm  max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm  eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this window, the largest statistic any of them returns, the range of temporal separations, and the depth of the deepest repeat. The elapsed times are the limitation rather than the depths: 4{} separations resolve at all and none exceeds 2\,d. All four were also tested in the visibility domain (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:visfit}) and against the interference signatures of \S  \ref  {app:rfi}. $^{a}$\nobreakspace  {}the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead. $^{b}$\nobreakspace  {}the archive indexes 200{} of the 404{} searched blocks by execution block and the rest only by member observing unit, so a separation within one unit cannot be resolved and is reported as under a day. $^{c}$\nobreakspace  {}the deepest repeat crosses the trigger, $T_\star =5.42$, but does not outrank its own window's controls and so does not reproduce a stage-1 event.}}{15}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:unattributed}{{7}{15}{The \NStageOneUnattrib {} stage-1 flagged windows with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics; $|\dot \nu |_{\rm max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this window, the largest statistic any of them returns, the range of temporal separations, and the depth of the deepest repeat. The elapsed times are the limitation rather than the depths: \NUnattSepResolved {} separations resolve at all and none exceeds \UnattSepMaxD \,d. All four were also tested in the visibility domain (Table~\ref {tab:visfit}) and against the interference signatures of \S \ref {app:rfi}. $^{a}$~the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead. $^{b}$~the archive indexes \NEbEpochExact {} of the \NEB {} searched blocks by execution block and the rest only by member observing unit, so a separation within one unit cannot be resolved and is reported as under a day. $^{c}$~the deepest repeat crosses the trigger, $T_\star =\UnattRepeatDeepest $, but does not outrank its own window's controls and so does not reproduce a stage-1 event}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Every stage-1 flagged window of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm  win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair; the remaining columns describe its deepest window, where $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest rest frequency of the masked species \emph  {in the topocentric frame}, as the released catalogue stores it. The disposition rule is applied in the \emph  {stellar} frame, after the frame chain of \S  \ref  {sec:frames}, with those offsets in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:bpicaudit}: for $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris they lie within 0.46\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic while the topocentric column reads about $-28$, the difference being the barycentric and systemic terms. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when the stellar-frame offset satisfies $|\Delta v|\leq 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise.}}{15}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:flagged}{{8}{15}{Every stage-1 flagged window of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair; the remaining columns describe its deepest window, where $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest rest frequency of the masked species \emph {in the topocentric frame}, as the released catalogue stores it. The disposition rule is applied in the \emph {stellar} frame, after the frame chain of \S \ref {sec:frames}, with those offsets in Table~\ref {tab:bpicaudit}: for $\beta $~Pictoris they lie within \BpicMaxAbsStel \,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic while the topocentric column reads about $-28$, the difference being the barycentric and systemic terms. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when the stellar-frame offset satisfies $|\Delta v|\leq \MaskVWidth $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Systematic uncertainty budget on $P_{90}$. The first block are independent errors on the quoted number and combine in quadrature; the second are carried separately: the sub-channel phase is an unknown about the transmitter, the decorrelation term is a \emph  {one-sided bias} that makes $P_{90}$ optimistic and cannot make it pessimistic, and the window-to-window term is a transfer uncertainty for the windows the campaign did not inject into.}}{16}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:p90budget}{{9}{16}{Systematic uncertainty budget on $P_{90}$. The first block are independent errors on the quoted number and combine in quadrature; the second are carried separately: the sub-channel phase is an unknown about the transmitter, the decorrelation term is a \emph {one-sided bias} that makes $P_{90}$ optimistic and cannot make it pessimistic, and the window-to-window term is a transfer uncertainty for the windows the campaign did not inject into}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The duty-cycle selection function, measured. Recovery probability against transmitter power, in units of $P_{90}$, and duty cycle $f_{\rm  dwell}$, from 3\,888{} injection trials. The abscissa is in units of $P_{90}$, which is the nominal trigger power multiplied by $\times 2.29$ for spectral response and smearing and then by $\times 1.2$ from the injection campaign, so a value of 1 here is $\times 2.81$ the trigger power. Recovery falls away below $f_{\rm  dwell}\simeq 0.3$. The dwell injections are zero-drift, so this selection function is measured for a stationary carrier and transferred to the drifting primary experiment; the joint drifting-and-intermittent cell of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:classcomp} remains unmeasured.}}{16}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dwellsel}{{9}{16}{The duty-cycle selection function, measured. Recovery probability against transmitter power, in units of $P_{90}$, and duty cycle $f_{\rm dwell}$, from \NDwellTrials {} injection trials. The abscissa is in units of $P_{90}$, which is the nominal trigger power multiplied by $\times \HanFacMed $ for spectral response and smearing and then by $\times \PNinetyOverTrig $ from the injection campaign, so a value of 1 here is $\times \PNinetyInTrig $ the trigger power. Recovery falls away below $f_{\rm dwell}\simeq 0.3$. The dwell injections are zero-drift, so this selection function is measured for a stationary carrier and transferred to the drifting primary experiment; the joint drifting-and-intermittent cell of Table~\ref {tab:classcomp} remains unmeasured}{figure.9}{}}
