\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Exchangeability of the 512-position control ensemble by stratum, over 1655{} windows. Boxes give the per-window control maximum, black diamonds the median on-star peak, and $p$ the Kolmogorov--Smirnov test of the per-window rank against $U(0,1)$. \emph  {(a)} by ALMA band, with the window count under each label; \emph  {(b)} by channelisation class, fine ($<5$\,MHz) against coarse. The ensemble tracks the on-star statistic in every band, and the fine class sits higher than the coarse, 5.79{} against 4.44, as its larger trial count predicts. Both depart from uniformity: fine $D=0.150$, $p<10^{-4}$; coarse $D=0.050$, $p=0.004$. The departure is not the flagged windows' doing --- removing all 71{} fine crossings lowers the statistic only to 0.103{} --- and \S  \ref  {sec:radial} identifies the radial noise gradient as the cause.}}{33}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ctrldiag}{{13}{33}{Exchangeability of the \NCtrl -position control ensemble by stratum, over \NWindows {} windows. Boxes give the per-window control maximum, black diamonds the median on-star peak, and $p$ the Kolmogorov--Smirnov test of the per-window rank against $U(0,1)$. \emph {(a)} by ALMA band, with the window count under each label; \emph {(b)} by channelisation class, fine ($<5$\,MHz) against coarse. The ensemble tracks the on-star statistic in every band, and the fine class sits higher than the coarse, \CtrlMedFine {} against \CtrlMedCoarse , as its larger trial count predicts. Both depart from uniformity: fine $D=\KsFineD $, $p\KsFineP $; coarse $D=\KsCoarseD $, $p\KsCoarseP $. The departure is not the flagged windows' doing --- removing all \KsFineCrossN {} fine crossings lowers the statistic only to \KsFineNoCrossD {} --- and \S \ref {sec:radial} identifies the radial noise gradient as the cause}{figure.13}{}}
